# Directory Mapping

- A directory mapping creates a directory map
- A directory map determins whether a user account or group will be created in the vault and the roles they will have
- A directory map links an LDAP group with one of the built-in Cyberark groups and determines how user accounts are created in the vault and the roles they will have
- You can edit these directory map later or create custom mappings according to the needs
- There are two types of map:

    - User Mapping
    Allows for authentication and defines user attributes, such as vault authorizations and locations

    - Group Mapping
    Makes LDAP groups searchable from within CyberArk, allowing mapped groups to be granted safe authorizations and to be nested within built-in CyberArk groups.

[Paste an image here]

> Prepare the Active Directory

- The first step is tocreate atleast four groups in active directory that will be mapped to predefined cyberark roles
- Request creation of 4 groups in LDAP:

    - CyberArk Auditors
    - CyberArk Safe Managers
    - CyberArk Users
    - CyberArk Vault Admins

- Once the LDAP integration is performed the user of any one of these four groups will be able to login in Cyberark with authentication and safe associated with each role

[Paste an image here]

> Predefined Directory Mappings

- The LDAP Integration Wizard is used to map AD groups to the four predefined CyberArk roles:

    - Vault Admins
    - Safe Managers
    - Auditors
    - Users

[Paste an image here]

> Vault Admins Mapping – Vault Authorizations

- The Vault Admins mapping is applied to any user who is a member of the LDAP group CyberArk Vault Admins
- LDAP users are provisioned in the Vault with the appropriate authorizations the first time the users log in Vault Admins

[Paste an image here]

> Custom Directory Mapping

- In addition to the predefined mappings, you can create custom directory mappings via a simplified wizard in the PVWA

